// The vent — a world with one small sun at the bottom of it.
//
// There is no daylight here. All the energy in the world comes from a point source that falls
// off by half every eight squares, so within about forty squares there is effectively nothing.
// A chloroplast is worth carrying near the vent and is dead weight away from it, and the
// carrying capacity is a disc rather than a slide.
//
// What that makes interesting is the edge. The centre is crowded and the periphery is empty,
// so the pressure at any moment is either to get closer — which everything else is also doing
// — or to find a way to live further out. Nothing here rewards either; both are simply
// available, and which one a lineage takes is the run's answer rather than the scenario's.
//
// Note what is *not* here: no chemosynthesis pathway distinct from photosynthesis. A
// chloroplast near a vent and a chloroplast in daylight run the same reaction on a different
// intensity. If chemosynthesis should be its own pathway, that is a chemistry change, and the
// chemical table lives in the scenario for exactly that reason.
//
//   mm-cli run scenarios/the_vent.ron --genome genomes/ancestor.mm --ticks 500000
(
    name: "the vent",
    seed: 20250812,
    width: 128,
    height: 128,

    light: PointSource(
        x: 64,
        y: 64,
        intensity: 4096,
        half_life_squares: 8,
    ),
    // Convection off the vent: a slow rotation that carries chemistry outward and keeps the
    // gradient from settling into a perfect bullseye.
    current: Rotational(strength: 64),
    fluid_interval: 1,

    seeding: [
        Uniform(chemical: 11, per_square: 409600),
        Uniform(chemical: 14, per_square: 409600),
        Uniform(chemical: 4, per_square: 409600),
    ],
    barriers: [],
)
